  "query_preview": "..Through Friday, High pressure aloft will dominate \nthe weather across northern Arizona. Surface pressure gradients \nwill favor northeast to southeast flow at 5 to 15 mph through \nThursday, mainly during the daytime hours. Nighttime winds will be\nmainly light and variable. Winds shifting to light from the south\non Friday. We're not anticipating much change in high and low \ntemperatures as the high center meanders over the southwest United\nStates. A very weak shortwave trough will brush the northeast \ncorner of the state from Tuesday into Thursday which will \nintroduce a bit cooler air aloft. The main impact will be that the\ncombination of lingering monsoon moisture and daytime heating \nwill act to produce some weak instability. The result will be \nisolated afternoon and early evening showers and thunderstorms \nover the higher terrain from the Mogollon Rim and White Mountains \nnorthward. \n\nOn Saturday...A low pressure system from over the Pacific will \napproach the western United States. The initial influence of the \ntrough will be cooler daytime temperatures and strengthening \nsouthwest winds. There will be just enough moisture for a slight \nchance of showers or a stray thunderstorm over the mountains. \n\nFrom Sunday into Tuesday or Wednesday...The operational GFS and \nECMWF forecast models and associated ensemble members have locked \non to a low of decent strength moving across the western states. \nBy Sunday and heading into early next week this low could tap into\ntropical moisture, portions of which will be the dissipating \nremnants of once hurricane Rachael. The main difference in the \ncurrent model package is that some solutions take the low center \nacross the Pacific Northwest and northern Nevada. Other solutions \ndrive the low center much further south with the center of the low\nmoving across southern Nevada and northwest Arizona. As might be \nguessed the further southward the low center moves the higher the \nprecipitation amounts across Arizona are likely to be. In either \ncase, much cooler temperatures and gusty southwest winds are \nnearly certain. It is a bit early to get too excited about \nprecipitation and much could change (this is the desert after all)\nbut definitely something worth keeping an eye on.",
